  "Name": "Clapham1969-PBS-a-HLH-m5",
  "Source": "P. B. Clapham, M. J. Downs, and R. J. King, \"Some applications of thin films to polarization devices,\" Appl. Opt. 8, 1965-1974 (1969). doi:10.1364/AO.8.001965 - Text p. 1967 (list of designs) and Fig. 3 caption (nH = 2.04, nL = 1.385, nG = 1.62, m = 5)",
  "Notes": "Cube polarizing beam splitter, design (0.5H L 0.5H)^5, compared in Fig. 3 for angular sensitivity of Rp\nSpecification: Rs high, Rp = 0 at 546 nm, 45 deg in glass.\nReported: Fig. 3 (computed, read only qualitatively): Rp = 0 at 45 deg; Rp rises most steeply of the three designs, to ~5 % within about +-1.5 deg of incidence at the cube face (the angle axis is measured outside the cube at the face, i.e. in air, refracted into glass). Text p. 1967: all three designs give comparable Rs for a given number of layers.\nNotes: Same media, angles and quarter-wave-at-operating-angle thicknesses as Clapham1969-PBS-a-*: H = 80.864 nm (half = 40.432 nm), L = 175.343 nm. 11 layers after merging adjacent half-layers: H/2, (L, H) alternating, L, H/2. lambda0 = 546 nm is assumed from the experimental section; the Fig. 3 calculation does not state its wavelength. The Fig. 3 angle axis is the angle of incidence at the cube face (outside, in air), so +-1 deg there is about +-0.6 deg in the glass.\nLayers from Text p. 1967 (list of designs) and Fig. 3 caption (nH = 2.04, nL = 1.385, nG = 1.62, m = 5); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours Rs 99.8 %, Rp 7.0e-6 at 546 nm, 45 deg in glass; paper Rp = 0 at 45 deg (Fig. 3).",
